.TH IO_SIGPIPE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
io_sigpipe \- Ignore the SIGPIPE signal

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/io_internal.h>

.IB void
.IB io_sigpipe()
.IB ;

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBio_sigpipe\fR function is an internal function used by \fBio_trywrite\fR and \fBio_waitwrite\fR. It is responsible for ignoring the \fBSIGPIPE\fR signal, which is typically sent when writing to a pipe where the reading end has been closed.

The function is designed to be called before performing write operations to ensure that the \fBSIGPIPE\fR signal does not cause the program to terminate unexpectedly.

The function is only relevant for non-Windows systems as it uses the POSIX \fBsignal\fR function to ignore the \fBSIGPIPE\fR signal.

The function is thread-safe and uses a static variable \fBisitdone\fR to ensure that the \fBSIGPIPE\fR signal handler is only set once.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBio_sigpipe\fR function in a write operation:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <lib/io_internal.h>

int main() {
  fd_type out = open("output.txt", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, 0644);

  if (out == -1) {
    perror("open");
    return 1;
  }

  io_sigpipe();

  const char* data = "Hello, World!";
  ssize_t bytes_written = write(out, data, strlen(data));

  if (bytes_written == -1) {
    perror("write");
    close(out);
    return 1;
  }

  printf("Written %zd bytes\n", bytes_written);

  close(out);
  return 0;
}
.nef